timestamp=2026-11-09T09:13:27.780Z level=INFO run=e6571189 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# 2025-03-04 is a Tuesday (isoweekday gives 2). So the billing period for each month starts on Tuesday (the same day of week as the earliest record).\n# That means the \"billing month\" boundaries are defined by weeks starting on Tuesday? Or simply that salaries are collected/pay periods align such that the period for each month starts on Tuesday?\n# The key phrase: \"the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record. On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\"\n# So each billing period is a month-long period, but it starts on that specific weekday. That means when aggregating hours/bonuses into \"billing months\", we need to bucket entries by billing periods. Billing periods are months that start on that weekday (Tuesday). \n# But in practice, how? Is the billing month defined as the calendar month that contains that Tuesday's start? Or are billing periods aligned to that weekday (like pay periods every 4 weeks starting on Tuesday)? That seems unusual. But the task also says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" and then describes the billing period starts on that weekday.\n# Another interpretation: for each calendar month, the salary is calculated, and payment/collection happens on the Tuesday (inherited from earliest record) of that month? Or the billing period in the month starts on that Tuesday.\n# Or maybe the \"month\" in \"monthly salary\" is not the calendar month, but a billing month that starts on the specific weekday (Tuesday) and runs for ~4 weeks? That would affect which entries go into which month bucket.\n# Let us look up how the data is structured across dates. The dataset spans March–June 2025. Let us see the distribution of entries by calendar month.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
