timestamp=2026-11-09T09:13:44.474Z level=INFO run=e6571189 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# 4 full calendar months, roughly equal entries. Also earliest is 2025-03-04 (Tuesday).\n# The question says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" - typically means calendar month. But then adds constraints about billing period starting on the same day of week inherited from earliest record, and employees collect salary on that day.\n# This might be specifying when salaries are paid/collected (administrative detail), but the calculation is still per calendar month. Or it might be defining the period boundaries.\n# Another interpretation: the \"month\" for billing purposes starts on Tuesday and ends on the Monday before next Tuesday? That would shift boundaries. Let us look at what dates fall where.\n# For example, March 2025 - the billing period starts on Tuesday. The earliest record is 2025-03-04 (Tuesday). So maybe billing month March 2025 starts on 2025-03-04 (Tuesday). Then the billing month runs from 2025-03-04 to the next Tuesday-start of next billing month (e.g., April starts on which Tuesday?). Or alternatively, for each month, identify the Tuesday that starts the billing period in that month - or the \"inherited\" start day means the billing cycle is anchored to Tuesdays.\n# Another possible reading: for the purpose of this dataset, the billing period starts on the weekday that was the weekday of the earliest record. So the start of the billing cycle is that weekday. That might mean we should bucket all entries into billing periods where each period starts on Tuesday. That could mean periods are 7-day chunks aligned to Tuesdays, or month-long chunks starting on Tuesdays. If month-long chunks starting on a specific weekday, that gives \"period months\" that don’t align perfectly with calendar months.\n# But the task says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" and \"the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record\". This sounds like a bit of domain language - perhaps the \"month\" in this context is the billing period (which is monthly but starts on that weekday). That changes bucketing.\n# Let us look at all dates and see how they cluster if we group by (year, month) of each date vs grouping by periods starting on Tuesday.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
