timestamp=2026-11-09T09:14:10.689Z level=INFO run=e6571189 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Let us also look at the full validation scoring logic more closely? The answer should map PESEL -> list of integer salaries, sorted by billing month in ascending order.\n# \"For each PESEL, the list of salaries should be sorted by billing month in ascending order.\"\n# So the ordering is by billing month. That suggests we need to identify which months appear as billing months in the data, and order them consistently.\n# Also \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week...\" - maybe the \"billing month\" key is the month whose billing period starts then. For example, if billing period in March starts on Tuesday 2025-03-04, then all work done from that Tuesday onward in that period counts toward March billing month? Or is it calendar month? \n# Let us look at the actual data ranges. Earliest is 2025-03-04 (Tue). If billing period for March starts on 2025-03-04, then that’s the start of March billing period. When does March billing period end? Possibly on the day before April’s billing period starts - which would also be a Monday? The next Tuesday after 2025-03-04 is 2025-03-11, 2025-03-18, 2025-03-25, 2025-04-01 (2025-03-04 + 28 days is 2025-04-01, 2025-04-01 is a Tuesday). So April’s billing period would start on 2025-04-01 (Tue). Similarly May 1st 2025 is a Thursday? Let us check: 2025-04-01 is Tue, 2025-05-01 is Thu, 2025-06-01 is Sun, 2025-07-01 is Tue. So May billing period starting on Tue would start on 2025-05-06 (2025-04-01 + 35 days = 2025-05-06? Or count Tuesdays: 2025-04-01 Tue, 2025-04-08 Tue, ..., 2025-05-06 Tue). Yes. June billing period starts 2025-06-03 Tue? 2025-05-06 + 28 = 2025-06-03, yes Tue.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
